WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.301
-Our first guest tonight
is an Emmy

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:04.070
and Peabody award-winning
producer,

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.439
a "New York Times"
best-selling author,

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:09.342
and the host of "Watch What
Happens Live With Andy Cohen,"

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.877
which airs Sunday through
Thursday nights on Bravo.

00:00:11.877 --> 00:00:14.514
Please welcome back to the show,
our good friend Andy Cohen!

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:15.882
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:24.590 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:33.265 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.265 --> 00:00:35.502
It's so good to see you again.
-It's so good to see you again.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:36.869
-I got to see you recently.

00:00:36.869 --> 00:00:38.538
I got to do your show
on St. Patrick's day.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.673
-That was so fun.
-It's so much fun.

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.708
-You didn't get drunk enough.
-You know, the thing --

00:00:42.708 --> 00:00:45.011
-Specially considering
the weight of the date.

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:47.279
-It was St. Patrick's Day,
and I'm always very excited

00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:48.915
to go to your show because I do
feel like it's

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:50.816
a professional excuse
to get drunk.

00:00:50.816 --> 00:00:52.084
-I do, too.
-Yeah.

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:53.886
-Yes.
-But you know, your show --

00:00:53.886 --> 00:00:55.621
and I say this as a huge
compliment --

00:00:55.621 --> 00:00:57.990
the pace is breakneck.
-It is.

00:00:57.990 --> 00:00:59.125
-It moves so fast.
-Yes.

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:01.060
I have a short attention span.
-Yeah.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:05.999
-So, it's about if I can keep
myself engaged for 30 minutes...

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:07.367
-Yeah.
-...then it's good.

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.135
That's why I'm always
changing the topic.

00:01:09.135 --> 00:01:12.505
-You know, it's like trying to
have a drink, I would imagine,

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:14.140
during speed dating where...
-Yeah.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.008
-...there's no time to actually
get the wine in your mouth

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:17.343
because you only have
five minutes to do...

00:01:17.343 --> 00:01:19.412
-Yeah. Somehow I'm able to get
the sips in.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.747
I know when to drink.
-You know what?

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.448
That's where I should -- I
should learn from you.

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:24.717
-Yeah, exactly.
-Last time you were here,

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.519
it was before the pandemic
and we were celebrating

00:01:26.519 --> 00:01:28.454
the 10th anniversary of
"Watch What Happens Live."

00:01:28.454 --> 00:01:29.789
-Yeah.
-13 years now.

00:01:29.789 --> 00:01:31.824
-13 years.
-Really amazing.

00:01:31.824 --> 00:01:36.128
-I know. I'm still -- I can't
believe we're still going.

00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:38.364
It's great. I'm so lucky.
-Yeah.

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:40.266
I mean, I feel the same way,
and we're at eight.

00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:41.834
-Yes.
-You know, and the thing --

00:01:41.834 --> 00:01:44.537
and again, going back to just
the way this show moves

00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.872
and the flow of it,
it does not seem like

00:01:46.872 --> 00:01:48.942
it's a show you could
do if you were bored with it.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:49.908
-That's true.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.908 --> 00:01:51.611
-Although,
have you hit a wall yet?

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:53.580
Was there ever a point
in your eight years

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:57.616
where you were like kind of
ticking a box?

00:01:57.616 --> 00:02:01.653
-I think m-- I will say
that if I got close to it,

00:02:01.653 --> 00:02:03.890
the pandemic reset the clock,
you know what I mean?

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:05.257
-Yes, of course.
-Because all of a sudden

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:07.894
you go back and you appreciate
everything a lot more.

00:02:07.894 --> 00:02:09.461
-You're like, "whoa,
I'm so lucky to have this."

00:02:09.461 --> 00:02:10.530
-Yeah.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:11.965
-Whereas right before
the pandemic,

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:16.168
I remember saying, like, "God!
I dare you to do something!"

00:02:16.168 --> 00:02:18.036
-Right, yes.
-[ Indistinct ]

00:02:18.036 --> 00:02:20.106
-Congratulations on your book.
-Thank you very much.

00:02:20.106 --> 00:02:22.508
It's not here anymore, yeah.
-Yeah, right.

00:02:22.508 --> 00:02:25.778
We are really enjoying it.
-This is your 3-year-old son,

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:28.147
Ben?
-Yes. I e-mailed you.

00:02:28.147 --> 00:02:30.016
I was a little upset because
there are a lot of

00:02:30.016 --> 00:02:31.918
words in the book.
-Yeah. You -- right.

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:33.119
-Yes.
-Going back to your

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:35.555
attention span.
-I lodged a formal complaint

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:39.993
with Seth because when he hands
me a super wordy book, I'm like,

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:42.628
"Oh, really?
This is what you want?"

00:02:42.628 --> 00:02:45.397
There is a book called
"Big Bug and Little Bug."

00:02:45.397 --> 00:02:47.000
-Okay?
-There is not even

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.603
an author credited.
It is such a pamphlet,

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:53.539
but he loves it and it's
literally like,

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:57.376
"A little bug
fell in a hole he dug."

00:02:57.376 --> 00:02:58.845
-Yeah.
-Next page.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:00.746
And I'm like, "This is --"
I'm like,

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:02.848
"Want to read
'Little Bug, Big Bug'?"

00:03:02.848 --> 00:03:05.050
-So you try to push -- you try
to push the simple stuff,

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.119
yeah, yeah.
The most heartbreaking thing

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:08.421
is when someone says --
when you write

00:03:08.421 --> 00:03:11.524
an illustrated children's book
that is 32 pages

00:03:11.524 --> 00:03:14.127
and someone said,
"We started it."

00:03:14.127 --> 00:03:15.428
-No!
-Yes.

00:03:15.428 --> 00:03:17.296
-Someone had said that to you?
-Someone had said to me,

00:03:17.296 --> 00:03:19.098
like, "We started it."
I'm like, "Started it?"

00:03:19.098 --> 00:03:21.200
-That is terrible. Wow.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.237
And when Jimmy Fallon said that
to you, you said what?

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:26.005
[ Laughter ]

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:28.508
-I said what I always say. I'm
like, "You're the man, Jimmy!"

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:30.209
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:33.446
"Whatever you say, boss!"
[ Laughs ]

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:36.749
Your mom, I know,
is a huge fan of your

00:03:36.749 --> 00:03:38.084
in your entirety,
your being.

00:03:38.084 --> 00:03:40.152
But does she still watch
the show 13 years in?

00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:42.454
-She does.
I mean, and she --

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:45.191
she has very mixed reviews
about my show.

00:03:45.191 --> 00:03:47.794
-That's so lovely.
-Always -- we had --

00:03:47.794 --> 00:03:52.197
her most recent gripe, which
really she was on for a while,

00:03:52.197 --> 00:03:57.136
we had Laura Linney and
Kristin Davis on one night.

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:59.272
-Very nice combo.
-Great actresses.

00:03:59.272 --> 00:04:01.774
And my parents are big
"Ozark" fans,

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:08.013
and we played a game called
"Have They Crapped Their Pants?"

00:04:08.013 --> 00:04:13.052
Where I showed them a celebrity,
and they have to say, guess,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:15.521
whether the celebrity
had, in fact,

00:04:15.521 --> 00:04:17.824
publicly admitted to crapping
their pants.

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:19.124
-Yeah.
-Fun game, right?

00:04:19.124 --> 00:04:21.427
-It is a fun game.
-There's a play-at-home factor.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:25.531
-Yeah.
-And my mom was so mad at me

00:04:25.531 --> 00:04:30.202
for for smearing Laura Linney
in that way and bringing her

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:33.039
down to my level,
I did not hear about --

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:37.543
the texts I got were so brutal.
And then, yeah, it was bad.

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:40.413
-Do you -- do look back at that
and think you should have

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:42.715
known better once you knew you
had someone your parents loved

00:04:42.715 --> 00:04:43.749
like Laura Linney?
Should you have, like,

00:04:43.749 --> 00:04:45.418
pushed "Crapped My Pants"
a show?

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:49.088
-No, because I think it's the
dichotomy of someone so

00:04:49.088 --> 00:04:50.890
brilliant that's Laura Linney...
-Yeah.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.491
-...and playing the game
with her.

00:04:52.491 --> 00:04:54.993
-You're right.
-I think, yes, no.

00:04:54.993 --> 00:04:56.162
I think we did the right thing,

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:58.530
and I'm sorry I upset my
mother.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.134
-But you put the show first.
-I do.

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:03.569
-I will say,
I think of all the interviews

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:04.937
Laura Linney has ever done,

00:05:04.937 --> 00:05:06.805
that's the only time she's
played that game. [ Laughs ]

00:05:06.805 --> 00:05:08.708
-You think?
-I think so. I think.

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:11.944
Hey, we --
it was so joyous to
us.

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:15.247
Anything that happens these
days that is just pure joy.

00:05:15.247 --> 00:05:16.849
-Yes.
-And even though this wasn't

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:19.184
necessarily positive --
You were pretty upset --

00:05:19.184 --> 00:05:21.854
but this is you and Anderson
on New Year's Eve.

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:23.622
You got a little bit...
-Oh, you have the clip?

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:24.957
-We have a clip, yeah.
-Oh, boy.

00:05:24.957 --> 00:05:26.492
-I mean, we -- I got to
be honest, Andy.

00:05:26.492 --> 00:05:28.160
We've shown it a lot.
We never get tired of it.

00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:29.862
And I would like -- here is --

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:32.398
You'll know what this is about
in a second.

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:36.368
-Watching Mayor de Blasio...
-Don't go on a rant.

00:05:36.368 --> 00:05:42.808
-...do his victory-lap dance
after four years

00:05:42.808 --> 00:05:48.314
of the crappiest term as the
mayor of New York...

00:05:48.314 --> 00:05:49.782
-That's how you want to start
the new year?

00:05:49.782 --> 00:05:52.952
-The only thing that Democrats
and Republicans can agree on

00:05:52.952 --> 00:06:02.661
is what a horrible mayor he has
been, so sayonara, sucka!

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:07.133
-I mean, I will say...
[ Applause ]

00:06:07.133 --> 00:06:10.436
...message aside, it is --
you two --

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:13.105
you and Anderson complement
each other so well,

00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:15.741
and that is my favorite thing to
watch is that two shot

00:06:15.741 --> 00:06:19.011
of friendship while you are --
and while he's just --

00:06:19.011 --> 00:06:20.246
you just felt like
he was stepping.

00:06:20.246 --> 00:06:22.147
-He was pulling my hood.
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:22.147 --> 00:06:25.518
-He was pulling me by the hood.
And what I forgot about

00:06:25.518 --> 00:06:29.088
that clip is how d-- well,
that I didn't forget,

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:30.889
but how hoarse I was.
-Yes.

00:06:30.889 --> 00:06:33.092
-Because I had really been
on my soapbox

00:06:33.092 --> 00:06:35.895
at that point
for four or five hours.

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:39.031
I was like, "And this is --
and Journey's fake!

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:41.700
The new Journey is fake!"
-It's so great.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:44.337
So when you hear someone
yelling that hoarse,

00:06:44.337 --> 00:06:46.104
it actually makes you feel
better about the fact

00:06:46.104 --> 00:06:47.206
you're getting yelled at
because you're like,

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:48.407
I think they do
a lot of yelling."

00:06:48.407 --> 00:06:50.009
-Yeah, exactly. I know.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:53.346
We got in the car after, and we
were leaving Times Square,

00:06:53.346 --> 00:06:55.581
and I went on Twitter
and I looked,

00:06:55.581 --> 00:06:58.450
and that clip was on Twitter,
and I was like,

00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:00.486
"Oh, this is this
de Blasio thing."

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:03.956
And I was watching it,
and I had fully forgotten

00:07:03.956 --> 00:07:07.260
or not realized that I said,
"Sayonara, sucka."

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.296
And I turned to Anderson, and I
go, "I said, sayonara, sucka?'"

00:07:11.296 --> 00:07:16.235
and he goes, "Yup. You did."
I'm like, "Oh, my God."

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:18.270
-It really is the perfect --
it's the perfect button.

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:21.607
-But by the way, has there
ever been a safer target

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:24.476
than trashing Mayor de Blasio?
-On New Year's Eve,

00:07:24.476 --> 00:07:29.715
in Times Square, everything.
-I do regret saying that he had

00:07:29.715 --> 00:07:31.951
been Mayor for four years
because it was a very long

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:33.252
eight years.
-Yes, exactly.

00:07:33.252 --> 00:07:36.789
-And I hate making an error,
and that was an error.

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:39.325
That was the one error I made.
-I think people forget it.

00:07:39.325 --> 00:07:40.760
I have a lot more
to ask you about.

00:07:40.760 --> 00:07:43.229
We'll be right back with more
from Andy Cohen after this.

